# Content Orchestrator — agent rules

Follow `AGENTS.md` at the repo root and `docs/AGENT_OPERATING_MODEL.md`.

## Never weaken

- Human Review Gate
- FORCE RLS / workspace isolation
- Spend fail-closed controls
- Provider abstraction
- Audit logging
- No TODOs / placeholders on production paths

## Working style

- Cursor is the default implementation worker.
- Work one scoped issue/work package per branch where practical.
- Read the active work package plus only the files needed for the task before broadening context.
- Highest business-value item from `docs/LAUNCH_BLOCKERS.md` first.
- No new frameworks; no speculative architecture.
- P0 frozen unless Critical defect with evidence.
- Alembic upgrade+downgrade for schema; linearize parallel heads before merge.
- Update launch/debt docs when closing a P0/P1 item.
- Prefer production-ready code with tests over incomplete scaffolds.

## Cost controls

- Do not perform full-repository audits during routine implementation.
- Do not ask a second AI worker to redo the same implementation unless independent review is explicitly required.
- Prefer targeted tests during iteration; rely on GitHub Actions for the full standard suite.
- Escalate to Codex/high-reasoning review only for concrete blockers, high-risk security/data-integrity work, complex migrations/concurrency, or milestone/release audits.
- Never run Cursor and Codex in parallel on the same implementation scope.
- Before escalating, record the exact failure, relevant files, attempted fixes, and test output so the specialist does not rediscover the problem.
- Handoff summaries must include scope, changed files, tests run, unresolved risks, and escalation status.
